TypeScript says process-style lookups always return a string. At runtime, a missing key returns undefined.

With an index signature, every key you read is typed as present:

const nodeEnv = env.NODE_ENV;
// string

Turn on noUncheckedIndexedAccess in tsconfig and the same line becomes:
// string | undefined

Declared fields like env.NAME stay string. Only keys reached through the index signature get undefined added, so the compiler now makes you handle the missing case.

My rule of thumb: I'd turn it on for new projects, and accept a few extra checks as the price of catching a crash at compile time instead of in production.

Has anyone turned it on in an existing codebase, and was the cleanup manageable?
